// ===================================================================
// sqrt.rho -- the square root of a rholang process
//
// Companion artifact to "Radical Fault Tolerance".
//
// The source process is a two-message race:
//
//    P  =  for( y <- x ) *y  |  x!( @"a"!(0) )  |  x!( @"b"!(0) )
//
// P has exactly two maximal reductions.  One exposes a barb on "a"
// and leaves a message on x carrying @"b"!(0); the other exposes a
// barb on "b" and leaves a message on x carrying @"a"!(0).  In no
// reachable state of P are both "a" and "b" exposed.
//
// PART 1 shows the naive homomorphic root doubled with itself.  It
// reaches a state exposing BOTH barbs, so it is not a root.
//
// PART 2 shows the coordinated root.  Each copy carries an arbiter;
// the two arbiters agree, so exactly one of the two barbs is exposed
// (twice), and the loser is parked rather than lost.
//
// Notation is rholang 1.4: `<-` is a linear receipt, `&` joins within
// a group, `;` separates groups.  Every multi-binding receipt below is
// a JOIN and must be written with `&`: with `;` the two arbiters can
// each take one half of what they need and deadlock.
//
// NOT EXECUTED: this file has not been run against an interpreter in
// the session that produced it.
// ===================================================================


// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 1.  The naive root, doubled.  BROKEN.
//
//   sqrt[ for( y <- x )*y | x!(Q1) | x!(Q2) ]
//     =  for( sy <- sx )*sy | sx!(sqrt Q1) | sx!(sqrt Q2)
//
// Doubling gives two receivers and four messages on sx.  Nothing
// forces the two receivers to take payloads from the same side of
// the original race, so the term below can expose "a" and "b" at
// once -- a state with no counterpart in P.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------

new naive in {
  contract naive( @copy ) = {
      for( sy <- @"sx" ) { *sy }
    | @"sx"!( @"a"!(0) )
    | @"sx"!( @"b"!(0) )
  }
  |
  naive!( 1 ) | naive!( 2 )
}

|

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 2.  The coordinated root.
//
// The channel sx is split into two tagged deposit channels, xl and
// xr, one per message of the original contention set.  Each copy
// deposits its own pair, so after doubling xl carries two messages
// and xr carries two messages.
//
// Each copy runs an arbiter.  An arbiter
//   (1) joins on one message from each tag -- so the two arbiters
//       partition the deposits between them, one pair each;
//   (2) contributes one bit to the shared channel `out`;
//   (3) joins on TWO bits from `out`, decides by a function that is
//       SYMMETRIC in the pair, re-emits the pair it consumed so its
//       sibling reads the same multiset, delivers the winner on sx,
//       and parks the loser.
//
// Symmetry of the decision table is what makes the two arbiters
// agree: they may read the two bits in either order.  The table is
// exclusive-or -- agree on Q1, disagree on Q2 -- which is also what
// makes the shared bit unbiased when either copy's local coin is.
//
// The local coin is a race, not a primitive: two messages, one
// receiver.  The nondeterminism the interpreter uses to resolve that
// race IS the copy's resolver, exposed as a term.  Arbitration is
// then the business of turning two independent resolvers into one.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------

new root, xl, xr, sx, park, out in {

  contract root( @copy ) = {

      // ---- the rooted receiver: the image of  for( y <- x )*y
      for( sy <- sx ) { *sy }

      // ---- the rooted messages, deposited under their tags
    | xl!( @"a"!(0) )
    | xr!( @"b"!(0) )

      // ---- this copy's arbiter
    | for( q1 <- xl & q2 <- xr ) {
        new coin in {
            // the local resolver, as a race
            coin!( 0 ) | coin!( 1 )
          | for( c <- coin ) {
              // contribute this copy's bit
                out!( *c )
                // read the whole multiset of contributions
              | for( e1 <- out & e2 <- out ) {
                  match ( *e1, *e2 ) {
                    // agree  => the left deposit wins
                    ( 0, 0 ) => { sx!( *q1 ) | park!( *q2 ) | out!( 0 ) | out!( 0 ) }
                    ( 1, 1 ) => { sx!( *q1 ) | park!( *q2 ) | out!( 1 ) | out!( 1 ) }
                    // disagree => the right deposit wins
                    ( 0, 1 ) => { sx!( *q2 ) | park!( *q1 ) | out!( 0 ) | out!( 1 ) }
                    ( 1, 0 ) => { sx!( *q2 ) | park!( *q1 ) | out!( 1 ) | out!( 0 ) }
                  }
                }
            }
        }
      }
  }
  |
  root!( 1 ) | root!( 2 )
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// What to observe.
//
// PART 1 can reach a state with a barb on "a" and a barb on "b".
// PART 2 cannot: both arbiters compute the same function of the same
// pair of bits, so both deliver the same payload, and the doubled
// system exposes one barb twice.  Two bits remain on `out` and two
// messages remain on `park`; both residues live on names outside the
// image of the root map, which is why the correctness statement is
// relative to the image contexts and not to arbitrary ones.
//
// Read as a protocol rather than as a term: `out` is a shared coin,
// the symmetric table is the agreement rule, `park` is the loser's
// place in the queue, and the join arities are the quorum.  The
// n-copy version replaces the pair by an n-fold join and the table
// by any function symmetric in n arguments; each copy then re-emits
// the n contributions it consumed, so a contention site costs n
// rendezvous and O(n^2) messages.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
